{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"With the two earlier findings consolidated, I moved to the configuration and participant eligibility mechanism, which nothing so far had touched, and found a contract mismatch worth a probe: AddVoter's captured comment says the given server is added as a staging server that the leader promotes once it is ready, while the AddVoter branch of nextConfiguration builds the new server with Suffrage Voter immediately, and Staging is deprecated and unused. Following the documented workflow — add the server, then bring it up — therefore changes the commit quorum at once, which in the adverse case leaves a cluster unable to commit, and for a single-voter cluster also unable to elect and unable to undo the change. The probe submits exactly that comparison: an address no transport answers is added through the exported AddVoter API to a single-voter cluster and to a three-voter cluster, and the events separate the applicability (the added server's suffrage, the voter count, the quorum, and whether the quorum exceeds the living voters) from the measured outcome (whether a later apply succeeded, the node's state, how many leaders remain, and whether RemoveServer for that server succeeded). Three comparisons declare the prediction, the control where the living voters still satisfy the quorum, and the claim that no in-band repair exists. Native feedback from the local dry run, explicitly not retained evidence: on the single-voter cluster the added server appeared as a Voter, the node ended as a Candidate with no leader in the cluster, and both the apply and the removal came back as not-leader with the configuration still listing two voters, while on the three-voter cluster the same steps left the leader in place with applies and removal succeeding. I also corrected one derived operand before submitting so the applicability reflects the quorum rather than a bare voter-count comparison.\"}"}}
